WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:08.740 --> 00:00:09.760
Good morning Daniel!

00:00:09.760 --> 00:00:10.760
Dan, please...

00:00:10.760 --> 00:00:15.349
I’m Marcia Boardman – we spoke on the
phone – and this is Philip Hart, our CEO.

00:00:15.349 --> 00:00:16.349
Hello!

00:00:16.349 --> 00:00:18.110
Nice to meet you both.

00:00:18.110 --> 00:00:21.760
So it says here on your CV that you’re interested
in rock climbing...

00:00:21.760 --> 00:00:23.029
Yeah... love it!

00:00:23.029 --> 00:00:24.730
Great – so do I!

00:00:24.730 --> 00:00:25.910
When did you last go climbing?

00:00:25.910 --> 00:00:28.750
Oh, err, well, it was a while ago now...

00:00:28.750 --> 00:00:29.750
Anywhere interesting?

00:00:29.750 --> 00:00:30.750
Yeah.

00:00:30.750 --> 00:00:31.869
I walked up the hill behind my house.

00:00:31.869 --> 00:00:33.800
Took me about thirty minutes!

00:00:33.800 --> 00:00:34.800
Oh.

00:00:34.800 --> 00:00:40.050
OK, erm, Daniel, can you tell us a little
bit about your current position?

00:00:40.050 --> 00:00:44.270
Certainly – as it says in the CV, I’m
part of the European Sales team at Networld.

00:00:44.270 --> 00:00:47.640
We’re the world’s leading supplier of
IT hardware.

00:00:47.640 --> 00:00:49.030
Why do you want to move on?

00:00:49.030 --> 00:00:51.570
Well, they’re too small for me.

00:00:51.570 --> 00:00:52.570
Networld!?

00:00:52.570 --> 00:00:54.110
Networld are too small for you?

00:00:54.110 --> 00:00:55.110
Oh yeah.

00:00:55.110 --> 00:00:56.350
I’m looking for something much bigger.

00:00:56.350 --> 00:01:02.739
Well, we’re not a big company by any means,
but we are looking to grow!

00:01:02.739 --> 00:01:05.030
What first attracted you to WebWare in particular?

00:01:05.030 --> 00:01:07.960
Well, I see WebWare as kind of like a stepping
stone...

00:01:07.960 --> 00:01:09.190
Sorry?

00:01:09.190 --> 00:01:15.070
Yeah, start here gain the necessary experience,
then move onto something bigger.

00:01:15.070 --> 00:01:19.229
OK... well, I do admire your ambition!

00:01:19.229 --> 00:01:22.219
Thanks – I’ve got lots of it!

00:01:22.219 --> 00:01:24.090
Let’s move on to managing people.

00:01:24.090 --> 00:01:28.640
Can you give an example of a time when you
had to deal with a particularly difficult

00:01:28.640 --> 00:01:29.640
managee?

00:01:29.640 --> 00:01:31.950
Sure – well I think that’s one of my strong
points actually.

00:01:31.950 --> 00:01:36.550
A couple of years ago, I was working with
a guy, he wasn’t pulling his weight, basically

00:01:36.550 --> 00:01:39.950
being lazy – so I told him to get out.

00:01:39.950 --> 00:01:45.869
Oh, you didn’t give him a performance review,
or a warning, or perhaps try to help develop

00:01:45.869 --> 00:01:46.869
his motivation?

00:01:46.869 --> 00:01:47.869
No, no, no.

00:01:47.869 --> 00:01:49.290
None of that rubbish.

00:01:49.290 --> 00:01:50.981
If someone’s not working hard enough, then
they’re out!

00:01:50.981 --> 00:01:52.590
That’s the way I work.

00:01:52.590 --> 00:01:54.569
I’m a hard man.

00:01:54.569 --> 00:01:56.130
I see.

00:01:56.130 --> 00:02:01.490
How would you handle a rapidly changing market?

00:02:01.490 --> 00:02:02.979
Basically not change anything at all!

00:02:02.979 --> 00:02:06.979
Just continue on as normal regardless!

00:02:06.979 --> 00:02:09.750
According to your CV you are fluent in Mandarin...

00:02:09.750 --> 00:02:14.260
Well, I wouldn’t say ‘fluent’ exactly...
but, I like Chinese food a lot – so I can

00:02:14.260 --> 00:02:16.240
read the menu!

00:02:16.240 --> 00:02:19.590
So you can’t actually speak the language?

00:02:19.590 --> 00:02:20.800
Erm, no.

00:02:20.800 --> 00:02:24.550
Can you tell us about a time you had to close
a particularly challenging deal.

00:02:24.550 --> 00:02:26.440
I haven’t had one.

00:02:26.440 --> 00:02:27.471
They’re all easy for me.

00:02:27.471 --> 00:02:31.810
I close deals all the time!

00:02:31.810 --> 00:02:35.370
OK – well do you have any questions for
us?

00:02:35.370 --> 00:02:36.550
No.

00:02:36.550 --> 00:02:37.740
Oh.

00:02:37.740 --> 00:02:38.920
OK.

00:02:38.920 --> 00:02:40.100
Erm.

00:02:40.100 --> 00:02:41.290
Thanks.

00:02:41.290 --> 00:02:44.580
Let’s move on to the presentation...

